\section{Methodology}
For the remainder of the manuscript, we focus on two-sample clinical trials with a continuous Normal endpoint and known standard deviation $\sigma$. 
Let $\theta_T$ and $\theta_C$ denote the true parameters of the treatment and control groups, respectively. The treatment contrast is defined as $\delta = \theta_C - \theta_T$,
where we assume without loss of generality that lower values of $\theta$ represent a better clinical response, thus $\delta > 0$ indicates a treatment benefit. 

We evaluate the one-sided hypothesis test:  
$$
H_0: \delta \le \delta_0 \quad \text{versus} \quad H_1: \delta > \delta_0
$$

Where $\delta_0$ denotes the clinically meaningful threshold or non-inferiority margin.  

In the Bayesian framework, the trial design evaluates this hypotesis using a posterior decision rule. 
The trial is declared a success (i.e., $H_0$ is rejected) if the posterior probability of $\delta$ exceeding $\delta_0$ surpasses a pre-specified probability threshold $\gamma$ (typically $\gamma = 1 - \alpha$):
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:an_prior}
\mathbb{I}\left( \Pr(\delta > \delta_0 \mid \mathcal{D}, \pi_A) \ge \gamma \right) = 1
\end{equation}

where $\mathbb{I}(\cdot)$ is the indicator function and $\pi_A$ explicitly denotes the analysis prior used to derive the posterior distribution.


