\subsection{Metrics to evaluate Bayesian Designs}
Frequentist Type I error and power are well-established metrics used to evaluate traditional trial designs. However, it is documented that when informative priors are incorporated into a 
design, strict point-wise control of the frequentist Type I error is impossible \citep{viele_use_2014, kopp-schneider_power_2020}. Consequently, in the Bayesian framework, alternative metrics are often evaluated, such 
as Assurance \citep{ohagan_assurance_2005} or Predictive Power \citep{micheloud_power_2022, spiegelhalter_monitoring_1986}.



All these metrics are special cases of a common metric $m$ \citep{best_beyond_2025}, which can be expressed as the expected probability of study success, integrated over a 
specified prior distribution for the parameter of interest $\pi_D$:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:m_general}
m = \int \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \delta\right) \pi_D(\delta) , d\delta
\end{equation}


It is crucial to differentiate between design and analysis prior \citep{ohagan_bayesian_2001}.  
\begin{itemize}
  \item The analysis prior $p_A(\delta)$ is the distribution used during the actual analysis of the trial to derive the posterior and evaluate the decision rule (see Equation~\eqref{eq:an_prior}). In this manuscript, 
$\pi_A(\theta_C)$ incorporates historical data via a Meta-Analytic-Predictive (MAP) approach, but other methods can be empolyed (see \autoref{subsec:info-borrowing})
  \item  The design prior $p_D(\delta)$, (see Equation~\eqref{eq:m_general}) is the assumed distribution of the underlying true parameter (data generating process) used exclusively in the planning stage to evaluate the design's 
operating characteristics across various plausible true scenarios.
\end{itemize}


When evaluating metric $m$, using the exact same prior beliefs for both the data generation and the inference ($\pi_D = \pi_A$) is known as calculating the 
fully Bayesian power \citep{micheloud_power_2022}. While this approach is considered the most logically consistent and natural by many Bayesian 
methodologists \citep{ohagan_bayesian_2001}, it may be not sufficient to regulatory authorities. Infact, a more comprehensive sensitivity analysis can be required, in order to evaluate the 
design under "prior-data conflict" or "drift" scenarios, where the prior information used for analysis ($\pi_A$) differs from the true data-generating process ($\pi_D$). 


\subsubsection{Prior on control}
When historical borrowing is applied exclusively to the control arm, the operating characteristics depend jointly on the true parameters $\theta_C$ and $\theta_T$. The general metric from Equation~\ref{eq:m_general} expands
 to a double integral over the joint design prior $\pi_D(\theta_C, \theta_T)$:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:m_joint}
m = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C, \theta_T\right) \pi_D(\theta_C, \theta_T) , d\theta_T , d\theta_C
\end{equation}


The traditional definitions of both Type I Error and Power impose deterministic constraints 
on the relationship between the treatment and control parameters. Specifically, we can fix the treatment 
contrast at a constant value $\delta^*$, such that $\theta_T = \theta_C - \delta^*$. Therefore, the joint design prior is reduced to a marginal prior on the control group, 
$\pi_D(\theta_C)$, collapsing Equation~\ref{eq:m_joint} into a single integral:



By varying the assumed true effect size $\delta^*$, we can define two metrics for evaluating the trial design:

\paragraph{Average Type I Error ($\text{avgT1E}$)}
When the true treatment effect is null ($\delta^* = 0$), we obtain Average Type I Error \cite{best_beyond_2025}:  
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:avg-type1-error}
  \text{avgT1E} = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C, \theta_T = \theta_C\right) p(\theta_C) \, d\theta_C
\end{equation}

\textit{Remark:} by defining $p_D(\theta_C)$ as a Dirac delta function centered at a specific value $\hat{\theta}_C$, Equation~\ref{eq:avg-type1-error} collapses 
exactly to the frequentist pointwise Type I Error at $\hat{\theta}_C$.  It is easy to notice that
the T1E is thus not a single constant, but varies pointwise as function of $\hat{\theta}_C$. 


\paragraph{Average Power ($\text{avgPower}$)}
When the true treatment effect is assumed to be a specific non-zero value ($\delta^* \neq 0$),  we obtain Average Power (at $\delta^*$):  
\begin{equation} \label{eq:avg-power}
  \text{avgPower}(\delta^*) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C, \theta_T = \theta_C - \delta^*\right) \pi_D(\theta_C) \, d\theta_C
\end{equation}


\textit{Remark:} We use the term "Average Power" rather than "Assurance" for Equation~\ref{eq:avg-power} to avoid confusion. Assurance often 
implies marginalization over a prior distribution for the effect size $\delta$ \citep{ohagan_assurance_2005}. Because our framework fixes 
$\delta^*$ and integrates solely over the control parameter space $\pi_D(\theta_C)$, "Average Power" prevents ambiguity.

Finally, consistent with the Type I Error case, specifying the design prior $\pi_D(\theta_C)$ as a Dirac delta measure $\delta_{\hat{\theta}_C}$ in 
Equation~\eqref{eq:avg-power} recovers the classical frequentist power. In this case, the power becomes a function of both the assumed true control mean 
$\hat{\theta}_C$ and the treatment effect $\delta^*$





%% As an alternative, it is also proposed to compute the maximum (or minimum) operating characteristic over a plausible subset of the parameter space, 
%% denoted as $\Theta_C$. Then this restricted maximum Type I error is expressed as:
%% $$\max_{\theta_C \in \Theta_C} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C, \theta_T = \theta_C - \delta\right)$$

\paragraph{Analytical Derivation of Average Power}

Finally, it has been proved the if the design prior coincides with the analysis prior ($\pi_D = \pi_A$), the design achieves exact asymptotic control of 
$\text{avgT1E}$ at the nominal level $\alpha$ \citep{best_beyond_2025}. 












\paragraph{Extending to Dual Criterion}
\note{Does this part makes sense? }
Let $\mathcal{D}$ denote the observed data. The single decision criterion is defined by a posterior probability threshold 
$\gamma_1$ on the effect size $\delta$ exceeding the margin $\delta_0$. The dual criterion adds a second requirement: the 
posterior probability that the treatment effect exceeds the Minimal Clinically Important Difference ($\delta_{\text{MCID}}$) 
must be at least $\gamma_2$. The dual indicator function $\mathbb{I}_{\text{dual}}$ is defined as:
\begin{equation} 
  \label{eq:dual_decision}\mathbb{I}_{\text{dual}}(\mathcal{D}, \pi_A) = \mathbb{I}\left( \Pr(\delta > \delta_0 \mid \mathcal{D}, \pi_A) \ge \gamma_1 \right) \times \mathbb{I}\left( \Pr(\delta > \delta_{\text{MCID}} \mid \mathcal{D}, \pi_A) \ge \gamma_2 \right)
\end{equation}
  
Where:$\delta_0$ is the non-inferiority margin (often $\delta_0 = 0$). $\delta_{\text{MCID}}$ is the threshold for clinical relevance ($\delta_{\text{MCID}} > \delta_0$). 
$\gamma_1$ and $\gamma_2$ are the decision thresholds (e.g., $0.95$ and $0.50$, respectively). Dual criterion restricts the "success" region in the parameter space.  Replacing $\mathbb{I}(\cdot)$ in Equations \eqref{eq:avg-type1-error} and \eqref{eq:avg-power} 
with $\mathbb{I}_{\text{dual}}$ yields:
\begin{equation} 
  \label{eq:avgT1E_dual}\text{avgT1E}_{\text{dual}} = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Pr\left(\mathbb{I}_{\text{dual}} = 1 \mid \theta_C, \theta_T = \theta_C \right) \pi_D(\theta_C) \, d\theta_C
\end{equation}
\begin{equation} \label{eq:avgPower_dual}
\text{avgPower}_{\text{dual}}(\delta^*) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Pr\left(\mathbb{I}_{\text{dual}} = 1 \mid \theta_C, \theta_T = \theta_C - \delta^* \right) \pi_D(\theta_C) \, d\theta_C
\end{equation}







\paragraph{Computational Framework}

Let $y_T$ and $y_C$ denote the sample means (sufficient statistics for Normal endpoints) for the treatment and control arms, respectively. 

Success is determined by a decision function $D(y_T, y_C) \in \{0, 1\}$, which evaluates whether the posterior distribution of the treatment contrast, 
  $\delta = \theta_C - \theta_T$, satisfies a set of predefined conditions. For instance, a standard design requires the posterior probability to 
  exceed a specific threshold: $\Pr(\delta \ge q_c \mid y_T, y_C) > p_c$ for specific critical values $q_c$ and probabilities $p_c$.

When evaluating multiple conditions simultaneously, such as in dual-criterion designs requiring both statistical significance and clinical relevance 
\citep{roychoudhury_beyond_2018}, the decision is formulated as the product of Heaviside step functions for each condition\footnote{Note that this product 
formulation naturally generalizes to an arbitrary number of conditions} $H(x)$, where $H(x) = 1$ for $x > 0$ and $0$ for $x \le 0$:

$$D(y_T, y_C) = \prod_{i} H\Big(\Pr(\delta \ge q_{c,i} \mid y_T, y_C) - p_{c,i}\Big)$$ 



The specification of priors, the sample sisez ($n_T$, $n_C$), and the decision function $D(y_T, y_C)$ uniquely define a conditional Decision Boundary 
$D_T(y_C)$ for each observed control outcome $y_C$,
$$D_T(y_C) = \max\{y_T : D(y_T, y_C) = 1\}$$ 
This represents the maximal threshold the treatment data $y_T$ must satisfy to declare success.

Given this boundary, we can compute the conditional probability of success $\Pr(\text{Success} \mid \theta_T, \theta_C)$ required in Equation \ref{eq:m}. Assuming
 true parameters $\theta_T$ and $\theta_C$, the overall probability of a successm is the expectation of crossing the boundary $D_T(y_C)$ over the sampling 
 distribution of the control data:
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:oc2S_general}
  \Pr(\text{Success} \mid \theta_T, \theta_C) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} F_T\left(D_T(y_C) \mid \theta_T\right) f_C\left(y_C \mid \theta_C\right) \, dy_C
\end{equation}
where $F_T\left(\cdot \mid \theta_T\right)$ is the cumulative distribution function of the treatment statistic evaluated at the decision boundary, and
 $f_C\left(\cdot \mid \theta_C\right)$ is the probability density function of the control statistic.

Because we are working with normally distributed endpoints, the sampling distributions for the sample means are normal: 
$y_m \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_m, SEM_m^2)$ for $m \in \{T, C\}$, where $SEM_m = \sigma_m / \sqrt{n_m}$. Consequently, the general integral in 
Equation \ref{eq:oc2S_general} can be evaluated directly using the standard normal CDF ($\Phi$) and PDF ($\phi$):
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:oc2S_normal}
  \Pr(\text{Success} \mid \theta_T, \theta_C) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Phi\left(\frac{D_T(y_C) - \theta_T}{SEM_T}\right) \frac{1}{SEM_C} \phi\left(\frac{y_C - \theta_C}{SEM_C}\right) \, dy_C
\end{equation}
 
