\subsection{Group Sequential Design}


In a GSD, the data are evaluated at $K$ interim analyses. Let $n_{m,k}$ denote the cumulative sample size at stage $k \in \{1, \dots, K\}$ for arm $m \in \{T, C\}$, where $n_{m,1} < n_{m,2} < \dots < n_{m,K}$.
Since we assume enpoints are continuous and normally distributed with known variance $\sigma_m^2$, the sufficient statistic at look $k$ is the cumulative sample mean, $y_{m,k}$ and $SEM_{m,k} = \frac{\sigma_m} {\sqrt{n_{m,k}}}$.


\begin{tcolorbox}
\note{I AM NOT SURE WHETER THIS PART is relevant. I just added cause it was the first idea I tried to implement}


\paragraph{Stage 1}
At the first interim analysis, the observed sample mean depends entirely on the true underlying effect $\theta_m$ and the initial sample size $n_{m,1}$. 
The initial cumulative mean follows a normal distribution centered on the true effect:
$$y_{m,1} \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_m, SEM_{m,1}^2)$$



\paragraph{Subsequent Stages}
If the trial continues to a subsequent stage $k$ (where $1 < k \le K$), the new cumulative mean $y_{m,k}$ is not entirely random; 
it is anchored by the data already observed at the previous stage, $y_{m,k-1}$. The final cumulative mean is a
weighted average of the known interim data and the data from the new cohort of patients, denoted $\Delta n_{m,k} = n_{m,k} - n_{m,k-1}$.
Given the prior interim result $y_{m,k-1}$, the expected value of the updated mean pulls the observed data toward the true underlying effect $\theta_m$:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:exp_val_stagek}
\mathbb{E}[y_{m,k} \mid y_{m,k-1}] = \frac{n_{m,k-1}y_{m,k-1} + \Delta n_{m,k}\theta_m}{n_{m,k}}
\end{equation}

Since the variance of the fixed historical data $y_{m,k-1}$ is zero, the variance of the new cumulative mean derives entirely from the new patients:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:var_stagek}
\mathbb{V}\text{ar}(y_{m,k} \mid y_{m,k-1}) = \frac{\sigma_m^2 \Delta n_{m,k}}{n_{m,k}^2}
\end{equation}


Consequently, the data at any stage $k$, conditional on the result of the preceding stage, follows a shifted and narrowed normal:

$$f_{m, k|k-1}(y_{m,k}) \sim \mathcal{N}\left( \mathbb{E}[y_{m,k}] , \mathbb{V}\text{ar}(y_{m,k}) \right)$$
\end{tcolorbox}


However, this formulation is computationally invonvenient to evaluate. \\

Another approach is provided in Chapter 3 of \citep{jennison_group_1999}. 
We can define the parameter of interest as the treatment contrast $\delta$.
 The sufficient statistic for $\delta$ at look $k$ is the observed cumulative mean difference:
$$D_k = y_{C,k} - y_{T,k}$$

The sampling distribution is $D_k \mid \delta \sim \mathcal{N}(\delta, V_k)$, where $V_k = \sigma_T^2/n_{T,k} + \sigma_C^2/n_{C,k}$. 
Let $\mathcal{I}_k = V_k^{-1}$ represent the cumulative information for the contrast. As we progress in the trial, this information will stricly grow: 
$\mathcal{I}_1 < \mathcal{I}_2 < \dots < \mathcal{I}_K$.
Let $D_{\Delta}$ denote the mean difference calculated exclusively from the new cohort enrolled between $k_1$ and $k_2$ (where $k_1 \le k_2$). 
Because $D_{\Delta}$ is derived from entirely different patients than $D_{k_1}$, the two statistics are independent:
$$\text{Cov}(D_{k_1}, D_{\Delta}) = 0$$

Trivially, $\mathcal{I}_{k_2} = \mathcal{I}_{k_1} + \mathcal{I}_{\Delta}$. Moreover, we can extend formula \ref{eq:exp_val_stagek} to $D_{k_2}$: 
$$D_{k_2} = \frac{n_{k_1} \times D_{k_1} + \Delta n_{k_1,k_2} \times D_{\Delta}}{n_{k_2}}$$
and by dividing numerator and denominator by $\sigma^2$: 
$$D_{k_2} = \frac{\frac{n_{k_1}}{\sigma^2} \times D_{k_1} + \frac{\Delta n_{k_1,k_2}}{\sigma^2} \times D_{\Delta}}{\frac{n_{k_2}}{\sigma^2}} = \frac{\mathcal{I}_{k_1} \times D_{k_1} + \mathcal{I}_{\Delta} \times D_{\Delta}}{\mathcal{I}_{k_2}}$$



The covariance between the earlier and later cumulative mean can be therefore written as:
$$\text{Cov}(D_{k_1}, D_{k_2}) = \text{Cov}\left(D_{k_1}, \frac{\mathcal{I}_{k_1} D_{k_1} + \mathcal{I}_{\Delta} D_{\Delta}}{\mathcal{I}_{k_2}}\right) = $$
$$\frac{\mathcal{I}_{k_1}}{\mathcal{I}_{k_2}} \mathbb{V}\text{ar}(D_{k_1}) + \frac{\mathcal{I}_{\Delta}}{\mathcal{I}_{k_2}} \text{Cov}(D_{k_1}, D_{\Delta}) = $$

$$ = \frac{\mathcal{I}_{k_1}}{\mathcal{I}_{k_2}} \left( \frac{1}{\mathcal{I}_{k_1}} \right) + 0 = \frac{1}{\mathcal{I}_{k_2}}$$

By standardizing the cumulative mean differences: $Z_k = D_k \sqrt{\mathcal{I}_k}$, we obtain the vector $\left\{ Z_1, \dots, Z_K \right\}$. Notably, it is a multivariate normal, since each $Z_k$ is a linear combination 
of the independent normal $y_{C,k} - y_{T,k}$ and marginally: $Z_k \sim N(\delta \sqrt{\mathcal{I}_k}, 1) $

Additionally, the covariance of the Z-scores becomes:
$$\text{Cov}(Z_{k_1}, Z_{k_2}) = \text{Cov}\left(D_{k_1} \sqrt{\mathcal{I}_{k_1}}, D_{k_2} \sqrt{\mathcal{I}_{k_2}}\right) = $$
$$ \sqrt{\mathcal{I}_{k_1} \mathcal{I}_{k_2}} \, \text{Cov}(D_{k_1}, D_{k_2}) = \sqrt{\mathcal{I}_{k_1} \mathcal{I}_{k_2}} \left( \frac{1}{\mathcal{I}_{k_2}} \right) = \sqrt{\frac{\mathcal{I}_{k_1}}{\mathcal{I}_{k_2}}}$$


It is finally possible to note that $\left\{ Z_1, \dots, Z_K \right\}$ is a Markov Sequence and the sequence of score statistics $\{S_1, \dots, S_K\}$, where $S_k = D_k \cdot \mathcal{I}_k$, 
can be viewed as a Brownian motion with drift $\delta$ observed at "information times" $\{\mathcal{I}_1, \dots, \mathcal{I}_K\}$.  
\note{cite: Lan and Zucker (1993) and J Whitehead - 1997}


Given this joint distribution, the standard and traditional way to evaluate sequential designs, can be found in Chapter 19 of \citep{jennison_group_1999}. 
Given the decision boundaries $(a_1, b_1), \dots, (a_K, b_K)$, computed following the chosen group sequential method (e.g. Pocock, O'Brien-Fleming \note{add citations}), where $(a_k, b_k)$ denote the continuation region at stage $k$, 
with $a_k$ the futility boundary and $b_k$ the efficacy boundary, 
the conditional operating characteristics can be computed as: 
  \begin{equation}
\label{eq:}
P_{\delta} \left( \bigcup_{k=1}^{K} \{ a_j < Z_j < b_j \text{ for } j = 1, \dots, k-1 \text{ and } Z_k \ge b_k \} \right)
\end{equation}

To compute this OC, we need to define the probability of exiting the trial at a specific analysis $k$, $\psi_k(\delta)$. 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:exit_prob}
\psi_k(\delta) = P_\delta \{ a_1 < Z_1 < b_1, \dots, a_{k-1} < Z_{k-1} < b_{k-1}, Z_k \ge b_k \}
\end{equation}


Evaluating Equation \ref{eq:exit_prob} directly, requires solving a $k$-fold multiple integral:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:multiple_integral}
\psi_k(\delta) = \int_{a_1}^{b_1} \dots \int_{a_{k-1}}^{b_{k-1}} \int_{b_k}^{\infty} f_1(z_1; \delta) f_2(z_2 \mid z_1; \delta) \dots f_k(z_k \mid z_{k-1}; \delta) \, dz_k \,dots dz_1
\end{equation}

where $f_1(z_1; \theta)$ is the marginal density of $Z_1$, and $f_j(z_j \mid z_{j-1})$ is the conditional density of $z_j$ given $z_{j-1}$  

As the number of interim analyses $K$ increases, direct numerical integration of Equation \ref{eq:multiple_integral} becomes computationally intractable.

Thus, the Markov property of $\{Z_1, \dots, Z_K\}$ can be exploited: the conditional distribution of $Z_k$ given $Z_1, \dots, Z_{k-1}$, depends only on $Z_{k-1}$. 

By defining $\Delta_k = \mathcal{I}_k - \mathcal{I}_{k-1}$, the conditional density is:
\begin{equation}
f_k(z_{k-1}, z_k; \delta) = \sqrt{\frac{\mathcal{I}_k}{\Delta_k}} \phi \left( \frac{z_k\sqrt{\mathcal{I}_k} - z_{k-1}\sqrt{\mathcal{I}_{k-1}} - \delta\Delta_k}{\sqrt{\Delta_k}} \right)
\end{equation}

Then, sub-densities are construced recursively. 
At the first interim analysis, the sub-density is simply the marginal density of $Z_1$:
\begin{equation}
g_1(z_1; \delta) = f_1(z_1; \delta) = \phi(z_1 - \delta\sqrt{\mathcal{I}_1})
\end{equation}

For subsequent stages $k = 2, \dots, K$, the sub-density is obtained by integrating the previous stage's sub-density over the continuation region:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:recursive_subdensity}
g_k(z_k; \delta) = \int_{a_{k-1}}^{b_{k-1}} g_{k-1}(z_{k-1}; \delta) f_k(z_{k-1}, z_k; \delta) \, dz_{k-1}
\end{equation}


Then, $\psi_k(\delta)$ simplifies to a sequence of K 1D integrals instead of K-dim integrals
\begin{equation}
\psi_k(\delta) = \int_{b_k}^{\infty} g_k(z_k; \delta) \, dz_k
\end{equation}




While this approach appears appealing, it is only possible to use it when teh prior on treatment is available. Infact, in that case, the boundary is a fixed 
constant on the Z-scale, while in the case of a prior on control, the boundary is a function of the observed data. Thus, we rely on Monte Carlo simulation to estimate 
the Operating Characteristics in that case (\note{I remember I read somewhere that they talk that in Bayesian is very common to rely on simualtions for OC}). 

