\subsubsection{Prior on treatment}
A conjugate prior-likelihood pair has the convenient property that the posterior is in the same distributional class as the prior. This property also applies to mixtures of conjugate priors.
This mean that assuming Normal likelihood and prior as a mixture of Normals, it is possible to extract closed formulas.


Extending the formula of Normal priors \citep{gsponer_practical_2014} is therefore straightforward. 

