\subsubsection{Prior on control}
As mentioned in the previous section, to estimate the Operating Characteristics we rely on a Monte Carlo algorithm.
Let $S$ be the total number of simulated trials. The initialization of the true control effect depends on whether we are evaluating predictive or conditional operating characteristics. 
For predictive OC, we draw the true control effect from the specified design prior $p_D(\theta_C)$, while if we are evaluating conditional OC, we fix the true control effect to a specific 
true value $\theta_C^*$. 

For each trial $s \in \{1, \dots, S\}$, the control effect is assigned as follows:
$$
\theta_C^{(s)} = 
\begin{cases} 
\text{drawn from } p_D(\theta_C) & \text{for predictive OC} \\
\theta_C^* & \text{for conditional OC}
\end{cases}
$$

The true effect on treatment arm is then deterministically obtained from the control arm by the specified true treatment effect $\delta$:
$$ \theta_T^{(s)} = \theta_C^{(s)} - \delta $$

For each trial $s$, the trajectory is simulated stage by stage exploiting the independent increments.
At stage $k=1$, the sample means are drawn directly from:
$$ y_{m,1}^{(s)} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\theta_m^{(s)}, \frac{\sigma_m^2}{n_{m,1}}\right) \quad \text{for } m \in \{T, C\} $$


At subsequent stages, we determine the number of newly enrolled patients, $\Delta n_{m,k} = n_{m,k} - n_{m,k-1}$. The sample mean is then sampled from: 
$$ y_{m,\Delta}^{(s)} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\theta_m^{(s)}, \frac{\sigma_m^2}{\Delta n_{m,k}}\right) $$

And we obtain the cumulative statistic for stage $k$ as a weighted average: 
$$ y_{m,k}^{(s)} = \frac{n_{m,k-1} y_{m,k-1}^{(s)} + \Delta n_{m,k} y_{m,\Delta}^{(s)}}{n_{m,k}} $$


Let $c_{T,k}(y_{C,k}^{(s)})$ denote the critical threshold for the treatment arm mean given a specific value of the control arm computed via root-finding (using \texttt{RbBesT}).
A trial stops for efficacy at stage $k$ if:
$$ y_{T,k}^{(s)} < c_{T,k}(y_{C,k}^{(s)}) $$

And the oppostive procedure is applied for dynamically computed futility boundaries.  \\
Let $I_{k}^{(s)}$ be an indicator function taking the value $1$ if trial $s$ crosses the efficacy boundary at stage $k$ (and has not stopped at any prior stage), and $0$ otherwise. Trials that cross either an efficacy or futility boundary are removed from the active simulation pool for subsequent stages. 

By aggregating the outcomes of the $S$ simulated trajectories, we obtain Monte Carlo estimators for the trial's operating characteristics. We can thus obtain probabilty of success at
stage $k$: $$ \widehat{P}(\text{Success at k}) = \frac{1}{S} \sum_{s=1}^S I_{k}^{(s)} $$
and also the overall probability of trial success:
$$ \widehat{P}(\text{Success}) = \sum_{k=1}^K \widehat{P}(\text{Success at k})  $$




For traditiional OC, where the control effect is fixed at a specific true value $\theta_C^*$, the estimator converges to the Conditional Power (or classic Type I Error in case $\delta = 0$):
$$ \widehat{P}(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C^*, \delta) \xrightarrow{p} \int P\left( \bigcup_{k=1}^K \{ y_{T,k} < c_{T,k}(y_{C,k}) \} \Big| \mathbf{y}_C, \theta_C^*, \delta \right) f(\mathbf{y}_C \mid \theta_C^*) \, d\mathbf{y}_C $$

Conversely, for \textbf{predictive OC}, the uncertainty around $\theta_C$ is incorporated into the design and the estimator converges to $E_{p_D(\theta)}[CP(\theta)]$: 
$$ 
\widehat{P}(\text{Pred Success} \mid \delta) \xrightarrow{p} \int \left[ \int P\left( \bigcup_{k=1}^K \{ y_{T,k} < c_{T,k}(y_{C,k}) \} \Big| \mathbf{y}_C, \theta_C, \delta \right) f(\mathbf{y}_C \mid \theta_C) \, d\mathbf{y}_C \right] p_D(\theta_C) \, d\theta_C 
$$

