\subsection{Application 1: Borrowing on the Control Arm}

We evaluate the design of a Proof-of-Concept (PoC) study in Crohn’s disease, an inflammatory bowel disease. The primary endpoint is the change from baseline to week 6 in the Crohn’s Disease Activity Index (CDAI), a 
continuous measure where lower scores indicate clinical improvement.

The original trial evaluated the efficacy of secukinumab against a placebo \citep{hueber2012secukinumab}. 
Let $\theta_C$ and $\theta_T$ the true underlying mean changes for the control and treatment arms, respectively. 
The treatment effect is defined as the contrast $\delta = \theta_C - \theta_T$, where $\delta > 0$ represents a treatment benefit.


To improve trial efficiency, the original design derived an informative analysis prior ($\pi_A$) for the placebo arm using historical data from 
six previous Crohn’s disease trials. To appropriately account for between-trial heterogeneity, the data from the 671 historical placebo patients 
were modeled via a Meta-Analytic-Predictive (MAP) approach and down-weighted to an effective prior sample size ($n_{\text{prior}}$) of approximately 
20 patients. Note that this example application was also analyzed by \citep{gsponer_2014} and \citep{best_beyond_2025}.




\paragraph{Decision Rules}
The trial evaluates success using the dual criterion formalized in Section X.X [Insert cross-reference to Methodology]. Specifically, at any given 
analysis, a successful terminal decision requires both strong evidence of a positive treatment effect and sufficient evidence of a clinically relevant 
effect size ($\delta_{\text{MCID}} = 50$):
$$
\Pr(\delta > 0 \mid \mathcal{D}, \pi_A) \ge 0.95 \quad \text{and} \quad \Pr(\delta > 50 \mid \mathcal{D}, \pi_A) \ge 0.50
$$

where $\mathcal{D}$ denotes the observed data up to that point. Furthermore, sequential designs in this application incorporate a Bayesian futility criterion. 
The trial can be stopped for futility if it is highly probable that the treatment effect is less than 40:

$$
 \label{eq:app_futility}\Pr(\delta < 40 \mid \mathcal{D}, \pi_A) \ge 0.90
 $$



\paragraph{Design Variations and Evaluation Strategy}
The literature presents varying structural implementations of this trial. The original protocol planned two interim analyses—occurring when 17 and 41 patients, 
respectively, reached the 6 week evaluation time point. Conversely, \citep{gsponer_2014} modified this into a two-stage group sequential design with a 1:2 allocation 
ratio (10 placebo and 20 experimental patients per stage). Taking a simpler approach, \citep{best_beyond_2025} evaluated the trial as a fixed design using 20 and 40 
individuals for the control and treatment arms, omitting the dual success criterion. Another cause of divergence is terminal decision mapping: while \citep{gsponer_2014} 
permitted a final designation of "indeterminate" by evaluating futility at the final analysis, we follow the original formulation, which restricts the final stage to a 
binary outcome (success or failure) by removing the terminal futility check.



To systematically evaluate the borrowing cost and the robustness to prior-data conflict, we will compute $\text{avgT1E}$, $\text{avgPower}$, and $\text{EUII}$ in the three design variations of this trial:
 \begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Fixed Design:} A single-stage design (analogous to the evaluation by \citep{best_beyond_2025})  
  .\item \textbf{One Interim Analysis:} An evaluation of the two-stage group-sequential design proposed by \citep{gsponer_2014}.
  \item \textbf{Two Interim Analyses:} An evaluation of the original three-stage trial protocol \citep{hueber2012secukinumab}.

\end{enumerate}

For each design variation, we evaluate a grid of analysis priors ($\pi_A$), a set of plausible data-generating design priors $\pi_B$, and a sequence of true treatment effects.

\paragraph(Analysis prior)
The analysis prior ($\pi_A(\theta_C)$) determines the degree of historical information incorporated into the final posterior decision. We specify a spectrum of seven analysis priors, ordered from full historical borrowing to no borrowing:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{MAP 1-Component:} A single Normal distribution obtained using MAP approach on historical data:    $ p_{\text{Norm}} = \mathcal{N}(-49,\,20^2).$
\item \textbf{MAP 3-Component:} A mixture of three Normals obtained using MAP approach on historical data: $ p_{\text{MAP}} = 0.51\,\mathcal{N}(-51,\,19.9^2)
    +0.44\,\mathcal{N}(-46.8,\,7.6^2)
    +0.05\,\mathcal{N}(-54.1,\,51.7^2)$
\item \textbf{Robust MAP ($\omega \in \{0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8\}$):} Four robust mixture priors constructed by assigning a weight $\omega$ to a vaguely informative component and $(1-\omega)$ to the MAP 3-component prior. 
The vague component is explicitly centered at the empirical mean of the informative prior (approximately $-49$, which reflects the historically expected placebo reduction in CDAI score). 
The weight $\omega$ acts as a tuning parameter: higher values shift mass to the heavy tails, yielding weaker dynamic borrowing and greater structural robustness to prior-data conflict.
\item \textbf{Vague Prior:} A non-informative prior representing a design with no historical borrowing ($n_{\text{prior}} = 0$), serving as the frequentist baseline: $p_{\text{vague}} = \mathcal{N}(-50,\,8800^2)$
\end{enumerate}

%% I am not using the advices of Weru since it is difficult to implement
\note{Should I say which ones are taken by Best?}


\paragraph(Design Prior)
The design prior ($\pi_D(\theta_C)$) represents the assumed true data-generating process for the control arm ($\theta_C$). To evaluate the design's sensitivity to misspecification, 
we evaluate four distinct design priors:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{(Dirac at $\theta_C = -49$):} to recover the classical conditional Type I Error and Power.
  \item \textbf{MAP 1-Component:}
  \item \textbf{MAP 3-Component:}
  \item \textbf{Skeptical Prior:} obtained from a stand-alone analysis of the historical study exhibiting the most extreme placebo effect: $p_{\text{skep}} = \mathcal{N}(-90,\,25^2)$
  \item \textbf{Misspecified Prior:} To inspect an hypotetical critical prior-data conflict scenario where the true control arm performs worse than historically predicted: $p_{\text{miss}} = \mathcal{N}(-90,\,25^2)$
\end{enumerate}


