We observe the well known effect that the power obtained with predictive methods is always closer to 50\% than the power obtained with conditional methods.
Infact, as showed in Table~\ref{tab:avg}, when the conditional power exceed 50\%, Assurance is lower, while under the null hypotesis ($\delta = 0$), or when
the treatment effect is not sufficiently large (e.g. $\delta = 40$), the average Operating characteristic is larger. 
\note{What is the mathematical reason for this?}

Figure~\ref{fig:GSPONER.plot} highlights a limitation of relying solely on pointiwise conditional operating characteristics. We obtain a Type I error of 1.2\% assuming
a fixed true value of $\theta_C = -49$, but the type I error varies based on our assumption on $\theta_C$ (and consequently on $\theta_T$). 
Since the actual parameter value is practically unknown, adopting the average T1E by averaging over the entire design prior, provides a more realistic 
assessment of the design performance, allowing us to account for uncertainty in $\theta_C$. 


<<GSPONER.plot, echo=FALSE, cache=FALSE, results='asis', fig.cap="Pointwise type I error across true control values. The red dashed line indicates the nominal 5\\% level and the blue line the average type I error.  The larger black point highlights the value used by Gsponer to compute the conditional type I error", cache.extra=file.mtime("../scripts/prior_on_control/1.2_Gsponer.T1E_and_avgT1E.control.R")>>=

pointwiseT1E <- gsponer$pointwiseT1E



T1E_col <- sapply(pointwiseT1E, function(x) x$Overall[["Power"]])
true_c <- seq(-91, 26, 3)

 
df_T1E <- data.frame(theta_c = true_c, T1E = T1E_col)

#also extract the avgT1E
avgT1E <- res.avg$delta.0$Overall[["Power"]]
  
ggplot(df_T1E, aes(x = theta_c, y = T1E)) +
  geom_line() +
  geom_point() +
    geom_point(
    data = subset(df_T1E, theta_c == -49),
    size = 4,
    color = "black"
  )+  
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = "dashed", color = "red") +
  geom_hline(yintercept = avgT1E, color ="blue") + 
  labs(
    x ="True control value (= treatment arm)",
    y = "Type I Error"
      ) +
      coord_cartesian(xlim = c(-91, 0)) +
  theme_minimal()
@




It is also possible to compute two different EUII, using conditional vs average OC. Results are reported in Figure~\ref{fig:GSPONER.plot.euii}.
The EUII computed with conditional OC, rely on the assumption $\theta_C = -49$, while the average EUII rely on a more realistic and less stringent assumption: $\theta_C \sim N(-49, 88 / 20)$. 

<<GSPONER.plot.euii, echo=FALSE, cache=FALSE, results='asis', fig.cap="EUII", cache.extra=file.mtime("../scripts/prior_on_control/1.2_Gsponer.T1E_and_avgT1E.control.R")>>=
euii <- compute_euii(res)
euii.avg <- compute_euii(res.avg)

df_conditional <- bind_rows(euii, .id = "P_H1") |> 
  mutate(Type = "Conditional")

df_average <- bind_rows(euii.avg, .id = "P_H1") |> 
  mutate(Type = "Average")


df_euii <- bind_rows(df_conditional, df_average) |> 
  filter(Delta != 0) |> 
  mutate(P_H1 = as.factor(P_H1))

  
ggplot(df_euii, aes(x = Delta, y = EUII, color = Type, linetype = P_H1)) + 
  geom_line(linewidth = 1) + 
  geom_point(size = 2) +
  labs(
    x = "Effect Size (Delta)",
    y = "EUII",
    color = "Metric",
    linetype = "Prior P(H1)"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
@


\paragraph{Varying prior}
Historical information is often heterogeneous and may not be fully compatible with the current stud, this is why a limitation can be the use of a Normal prior, 
sensitive to prior-data conflict.
It is therefore natural to consider more flexible prior specifications based on mixture distributions:


\begin{itemize}
      \item \textbf{Vague (flat) prior.}  
    $p_{\text{vague}} = \mathcal{N}(-50,\,8800^2)$

    \item \textbf{MAP prior} obtained by MAP approach on six previous Crohn’s disease trials.   
    $ p_{\text{MAP}} = 0.51\,\mathcal{N}(-51,\,19.9^2)
    +0.44\,\mathcal{N}(-46.8,\,7.6^2)
    +0.05\,\mathcal{N}(-54.1,\,51.7^2)$

    \item \textbf{Robust MAP prior with weight $0.2$.}  
    Robustified version of MAP using a non informative component corresponding to 1 observation with weight $0.2$, leading to a prior where $80\%$ of the weight is assigned to the informative MAP component and $20\%$ to the robust component.

    \item \textbf{Robust MAP prior with weight $0.5$.}  
        Robustified version of MAP using a non informative component centered at $-50$ with weight $0.5$,

    \item \textbf{Skeptical prior.} obtained from a stand-alone analysis of the historical study exhibiting the most extreme placebo effect.
    $p_{\text{skep}} = \mathcal{N}(-90,\,25^2)$
    \item \textbf{Normal}, the assumed prior by Gsponer
   $ \mathcal{N}(-49,\,20^2).$

    \item \textbf{Robustified MAP priors using heavy-tailed distributions} following \citep{weru_information_2026}, we also inspect the use of an approximated t distribution as robust component:
    $t_3(-50,\,88)$ by a mixture of 15 Normals (using w = 0.2 and w = 0.5)
\end{itemize}

Note that the first 3 priors are the same evalauted in \citet{best_beyond_2025}:


\paragraph{Classic T1E with varying prior}
Figure~\ref{fig:GSPONER.plot.T1E} reports the Type I error obtained when the true 
control effect is fixed at $\theta_C=-49$. 
%MAP prior and the mildly robustified priors ($w=0.2$) keep Type I error well below the nominal $5\%$ threshold. 
Increasing the robustness weight decreases 
the amount of borrowing and leads to slightly more conservative behavior, while the vague prior produces larger Type I error values due to the absence of historical 
information. The skeptical prior is the most conservative specification, resulting in extremely small Type I error since the use of an historical value
for the control really extreme, lead to a generally reduced possibility of declaring success. 

However, as Figure~\ref{fig:GSPONER.plot.pointwiseT1E} shows, as $\theta_C$ drifts away from the prior center, robustified priors maintain better T1E 
stability compared to strictly informative priors

These results also illustrate a practical difficulty of evaluating Bayesian adaptive designs using pointwise Type I error alone. For each prior specification, 
Type I error must be assessed over a range of plausible control values, and additional evaluations of power are required for each treatment effect scenario. 
This leads to a very large number of operating scenarios to inspect. The use of average Type I error can therefore provide a more concise summary of 
the frequentist properties of the design by reducing the evaluation to a single measure for each prior specification, while assurance can still be studied
separately across different treatment effect assumptions.

Finally, note that the Operating Characteristis obtained using a vague Normal or a t-distribution as Robust component, are practically the same, see Figure~\ref{fig:GSPONER.plot.pointwiseT1E}.
For easier reporting we will thus focus on classic Robust component \note{Maybe put results using t-distr in appendix}.



<<GSPONER.prep_priors, echo=FALSE, cache=FALSE, results='asis', cache.extra=file.mtime("../scripts/prior_on_control/1.3_one.interim.T1E.inspection.R")>>=

t1e_seq <- readRDS("../data/T1E.sequential.rds")

results_fixed <- t1e_seq$results_fixed
results_grid <- t1e_seq$results_grid


prior_colours <- c(
  MAP            = "#2f00ff",

  Normal         = "#0099cc",

  Vague          = "#8d7f7f",
  Skeptical      = "#232323",

  Robust_0.20    = "#ff0000",
  Robust_0.5     = "#ffa600",

  Robust_t_0.20  = "darkgreen",
  Robust_t_0.5   = "#11ff00"
)
@




<<GSPONER.plot.T1E, echo=FALSE, cache=FALSE, results='asis', fig.cap="T1E varying priors, assuming $\theta_c = -49$", cache.extra=file.mtime("../scripts/prior_on_control/1.3_one.interim.T1E.inspection.R")>>=

t1e_df <- results_fixed |> filter(Delta == 0)

t1e_df |>
  ggplot(aes(x = Prior, y = Power, fill = Prior)) +
  geom_col(width = 0.6) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey40", linewidth = 0.6) +
  geom_text(aes(label = sprintf("%.1f%%", 100 * Power)),
            vjust = -0.4, size = 3.5) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = prior_colours) +
  scale_y_continuous(labels = scales::percent_format(accuracy = 1),
                     limits = c(0, max(t1e_df$Power) * 1.25)) +
  labs(
    x        = "Control prior",
    y        = "T1E",
    fill     = "Control prior"
  ) +
  theme_bw(base_size = 11) +
  theme(legend.position = "none",    axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 30, hjust = 1)

)



@


<<GSPONER.plot.pointwiseT1E, echo=FALSE, cache=FALSE, results='asis', fig.cap="Pointwise T1E for varying priors", cache.extra=file.mtime("../scripts/prior_on_control/1.3_one.interim.T1E.inspection.R")>>=

null_diagonal <- results_grid |> 
  filter(Theta_t == Theta_c)

ggplot(null_diagonal, aes(x = Theta_c, y = Power, color = Prior, group = Prior)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.8) +
  geom_point(size = 2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = "dashed", color = "black") +
  scale_y_continuous(labels = scales::percent, breaks = seq(0, 0.4, by = 0.05)) +
  labs(
    x = "True Control Value (= treatment arm)",
    y = "T1E"
  ) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom")
@



%\paragraph{Important discussion on the actual desing}
%I now suggest to stop and really understand the design choices made. From my perspective, there are two different ideas on how the 
%designers created the clinical trial:

%\begin{itemize}
%  \item IDEA 1: the point of adding the futility stop is only to calibrate the design such that the T1E is not inflated, but 
%  value of the estimated delta below the futility treshold (in this case $\delta_{fut} = 40$) and above the significant treshold $\delta_{sign} = 0$ also are considered a success of the trial. 
%  Then, e.g. an estimated value of $\delta = 20$ still means the drug is effectively working, then adding the clinical relevance treshold $\delta_{rel} = 50$ results in power for $\delta < 50$ that 
%  cannot be larger than 50\% by construction of DC. This is really counterintutive. 

%  \item IDEA 2: the success is only declared if the estimated $\delta > 40$ with high confidence, then the choice of using a DC makes sense, but evaluating the T1E only at $\delta = 0$ not much, 
%   Since a false positive would also come up if for example delta = 30. So we should use the worst case scenario T1E: 
%   $\sup_{\delta \in H_0} \mathbb{P}(\text{success} \mid \delta)$

%\end{itemize}



\paragraph{Important discussion on the actual design}
It is important to highlight how the Dual Criterion works in the context of hypotesis testing. 
\citep{roychoudhury_beyond_2018} keeps the standard statistical null hypothesis unchanged (no treatment effect) but modifies the definition of success by additionally requiring clinical relevance. 
Hence, the DC framework does not redefine the null hypothesis, but instead restricts the rejection region by introducing a clinically motivated decision value.

For example, in this case study, effects satisfying $0 < \delta < 40$ correspond to situations where the treatment has a positive effect, but the effect may be considered clinically insufficient. 
Declaring success in this region is not a Type I error, since the null hypothesis is false, however, from a clinical perspective, advancing a treatment with such a small effect may still be undesirable. 

By construction, the power if $\delta =$ DV is approximately $50\%$, so for values $\delta \in [0, 40]$ Power cannot be larger that 50\%, nonetheless \citep{roychoudhury_beyond_2018} emphasizes that operating characteristics 
should not only be evaluated at the null value, but across the full parameter space. 






<<GSPONER.prep_avgpriors, echo=FALSE, cache=FALSE, results='asis', cache.extra=file.mtime("../scripts/prior_on_control/1.4_one.interim_avgT1E_inspection.R")>>=
all_results <- readRDS("../data/1.6.rds")


euii <- lapply(all_results, function(dec.func) {
  lapply(dec.func, function(job) {
    compute_euii(job)
  })
})


# trasform "ap.MAP_dp.Robust_0.20" -> list(ap, dp) 
parse_job_name <- function(job_name) {
  ap <- sub("^ap\\.(.+)_dp\\..+$", "\\1", job_name)
  dp <- sub("^ap\\..+_dp\\.(.+)$", "\\1", job_name)
  list(ap = ap, dp = dp)
}


# -- Flatten $Overall (power) across all decision lists / jobs / deltas --
flatten_power <- function(all_results) {
  bind_rows(lapply(names(all_results), function(dec_name) {
    dec_res <- all_results[[dec_name]]
    bind_rows(lapply(names(dec_res), function(job_name) {
      parsed <- parse_job_name(job_name)
      job    <- dec_res[[job_name]]        # named list: delta.0, delta.10, ...
      bind_rows(lapply(names(job), function(delta_name) {
        d  <- as.numeric(sub("delta\\.", "", delta_name))
        ov <- job[[delta_name]]$Overall
        data.frame(
          decision_list  = dec_name,
          analysis_prior = parsed$ap,
          design_prior   = parsed$dp,
          delta          = d,
          as.data.frame(t(ov)),
          check.names    = FALSE
        )
      }))
    }))
  }))
}


# -- Flatten EUII across all decision lists / jobs / prior_H1 values --
flatten_euii <- function(euii) {
  bind_rows(lapply(names(euii), function(dec_name) {
    dec_res <- euii[[dec_name]]
    bind_rows(lapply(names(dec_res), function(job_name) {
      parsed   <- parse_job_name(job_name)
      job_euii <- dec_res[[job_name]]      # named list: "0.01", "0.1", "0.5"
      bind_rows(lapply(names(job_euii), function(prior_h1) {
        job_euii[[prior_h1]] |>
          mutate(
            decision_list  = dec_name,
            analysis_prior = parsed$ap,
            design_prior   = parsed$dp,
            prior_H1       = as.numeric(prior_h1)
          )
      }))
    }))
  }))
}

df_power <- flatten_power(all_results)
df_euii  <- flatten_euii(euii)


# Not use skeptical as analysiis prior since this is what Best did, but 
#also cause we would never use the skeptical as historical
df_power <- df_power  |> filter (analysis_prior != "Skeptical")
df_euii <- df_euii  |> filter (analysis_prior != "Skeptical")




# rename different decisions
dec_labels <- c(
  "decision_list"             = "Classic",
  "decision_list_nofut"       = "No Futility",
  "decision_list_norel"       = "No Relevance",
  "decision_list_nofut.norel" = "No Futility No Relevance"
)

DEC_LEVELS <- c("Classic", "No Futility", "No Relevance", "No Futility No Relevance")

df_power <- df_power |>
  mutate(decision_list = dplyr::recode(decision_list, !!!dec_labels),
         decision_list = factor(decision_list, levels = DEC_LEVELS))  |> 
         select(decision_list, analysis_prior, design_prior, delta, Power)

df_euii <- df_euii |>
  mutate(decision_list = dplyr::recode(decision_list, !!!dec_labels),
         decision_list = factor(decision_list, levels = DEC_LEVELS))

AP_LEVELS  <- c("MAP", "Normal", "Robust_0.20", "Robust_0.5", "Vague")
DP_LEVELS  <- c("MAP", "Normal", "Robust_0.20", "Robust_0.5", "Vague", "Skeptical")

df_power <- df_power |>
  mutate(analysis_prior = factor(analysis_prior, levels = AP_LEVELS),
         design_prior   = factor(design_prior,   levels = DP_LEVELS))

df_euii <- df_euii |>
  mutate(analysis_prior = factor(analysis_prior, levels = AP_LEVELS),
         design_prior   = factor(design_prior,   levels = DP_LEVELS))



df_classic <- df_power  |> filter (
  decision_list == "Classic", delta != 90)

@



<<GSPONER.plot.avgT1E, echo=FALSE, cache=FALSE, results='asis', fig.width=10, fig.height=7, fig.cap="Average Operating characterstics for varying desing and analysis priors", cache.extra=file.mtime("../scripts/prior_on_control/1.4_one.interim_avgT1E_inspection.R")>>=
df_classic |>
  ggplot(aes(x = design_prior, y = analysis_prior, fill = Power)) +
  geom_tile(colour = "white", linewidth = 0.5) +
  geom_text(aes(label = sprintf("%.3f", Power)), size = 2.8) +
  scale_fill_gradient2(
    low      = "#2E6DA4",
    mid      = "white",
    high     = "#E04F39",
    midpoint = 0.5,
    labels   = scales::percent_format(accuracy = 0.1),
    name     = "Power"
  ) +
  facet_wrap(~ delta, ncol = 3,
             labeller = labeller(delta = function(x) paste0("delta = ", x))) +
  labs(  x = "Design Prior",
    y = "Analysis Prior"
  ) +
  theme_bw(base_size = 11) +
  theme(
    axis.text.x      = element_text(angle = 35, hjust = 1),
    panel.grid       = element_blank(),
    strip.background = element_rect(fill = "grey92")
  )
@


<<GSPONER.plot.Assurance, echo=FALSE, cache=FALSE, results='asis', fig.width=10, fig.height=7, fig.cap="Average Operating characterstics, facet by design prior, colours analysis priors", cache.extra=file.mtime("../scripts/prior_on_control/1.4_one.interim_avgT1E_inspection.R")>>=
df_classic |>
  ggplot(aes(
    x = delta,
    y = Power,
    colour = analysis_prior,
    group = analysis_prior
  )) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1) +
  geom_point(size = 2) +
  facet_wrap(
    ~ design_prior,
    ncol = 3
  ) +
  scale_y_continuous(
    labels = scales::percent_format(accuracy = 0.1)
  ) +
  labs(
    x = expression(delta),
    y = "Power",
    colour = "Analysis Prior"
  ) +
  theme_bw(base_size = 11) +
  theme(
    panel.grid.minor = element_blank(),
    strip.background = element_rect(fill = "grey92"),
    legend.position = "bottom"
  )
@

\paragraph{Final Analysis}
The results in Figure~\ref{fig:GSPONER.plot.avgT1E} and Figure~\ref{fig:GSPONER.plot.Assurance} suggest that the Design Prior has more influence than the Analysis Prior on Assurance ($\delta > 0$). At $\delta = 0$, $avgT1E$ is controlled (2.5\% to 6.6\%) for MAP, Robust, and Normal design priors, with 
the MAP-MAP design resulting in an avgt1E at 2.5\%. 
Notably, the Vague design prior produces an assurance of nearly 50\% across all $\delta$ values. Conversely, the Skeptical design prior inflates $at1e$ up to 22\% under the null, as it places substantial mass on large control arm responses

