\hrule
\medskip

\note{In the paper from Best, when they have prior on control, 
 they focus on the avgT1e. They don't mention at all how they would compute Assurance (or Power) in that situation. Therefore I assumed the correct version was using 
 a deterministic $\delta^*$, but maybe we should use a distribution $p(\delta)$}.
 
INFACT: 
What I am computing is:
$$\text{Power}(\delta^*) = \int Pr(\text{Study success} | \theta_c, \theta_t = \theta_c + \delta^*) p(\theta_c) d\theta_c$$

where $\delta^*$ is fixed, 
while \textbf{REAL ASSURANCE} should be:
$$\text{Assurance} = \iint Pr(\text{Study success} | \theta_c, \theta_t = \theta_c + \delta) p(\theta_c) p(\delta) d\theta_c d\delta$$
where we would need a design prior on $\delta$ ($p(\delta)$) which we actually don't have, but can use skeptical or some other distirbutions.

